<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jimin Xiao
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Liverpool
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:jimin.xiao@xjtlu.edu.cn">jimin.xiao@xjtlu.edu.cn</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have expanded reasoning capabilities into 3D domains, enabling fine-grained spatial understanding. However, the substantial size of 3D MLLMs and the high dimensionality of input features introduce considerable inference overhead, which limits practical deployment on resource constrained platforms. To overcome this limitation, this paper presents Efficient3D, a unified framework for visual token pruning that accelerates 3D MLLMs while maintaining competitive accuracy. The proposed framework introduces a Debiased Visual Token Importance Estimator (DVTIE) module, which considers the influence of shallow initial layers during attention aggregation, thereby producing more reliable importance predictions for visual tokens. In addition, an Adaptive Token Rebalancing (ATR) strategy is developed to dynamically adjust pruning strength based on scene complexity, preserving semantic completeness and maintaining balanced attention across layers.
Together, they enable context-aware token reduction that maintains essential semantics with lower computation.
Comprehensive experiments conducted on five representative 3D vision and language benchmarks, including ScanRefer, Multi3DRefer, Scan2Cap, ScanQA, and SQA3D, demonstrate that Efficient3D achieves superior performance compared with unpruned baselines, with a +2.57% CIDEr improvement on the Scan2Cap dataset. Therefore, Efficient3D provides a scalable and effective solution for efficient inference in 3D MLLMs. The code is released at: https://github.com/sol924/Efficient3D</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> have made remarkable progress, driving evolution of 3D MLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> toward enhanced spatial understanding and reasoning capabilities. At present, 3D MLLMs have demonstrated remarkable advancements across tasks such as referred object grounding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and dense scene captioning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, substantially enhancing the capability of language models for 3D perception and reasoning. Although 3D MLLMs have achieved impressive performance, their large model size makes them impractical for deployment on many embedded or resource-constrained devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>. Compared with conventional MLLMs, the scalability issue becomes even more pronounced in 3D MLLMs due to the higher data dimensionality and increased computational burden.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02689v1/introduction_feature_upload.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/543;" width="685" height="543" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
(a) illustrates that the initial layers in the 3D MLLM focus more on visual tokens on ScanRefer using LLaVA-1.5-7B. (b) compares global prediction and DVTIE in terms of correctly pruned visual tokens. (c) shows visual token pruning under simple and complex questions, where simple questions typically involve a single object, while complex questions involve multiple objects.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In conventional MLLMs, token pruning has emerged as an effective inference acceleration strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, which eliminates redundant visual tokens to reduce computational and memory overhead, thereby providing a promising pathway to alleviate the computational overhead in 3D MLLMs. For example, Fast3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> observes that global attention patterns in existing 3D MLLMs can effectively indicate non-essential tokens in 3D contexts. Building on this insight, it estimates token importance by averaging attention maps across all backbone layers, achieving substantial acceleration with minimal performance degradation. However, such averaging may lead the pruning process in deeper layers of 3D MLLMs to be overly affected by the attention patterns of the initial layer. As illustrated in Fig. 1 (a), where layer visual importance denotes the average attention assigned to visual tokens in each MLLM layer by considering both text-to-vision attention and visual self-attention, the importance of visual tokens is concentrated in the initial two layers. If the average is taken directly, the importance of the non-essential tokens in deep layers will be exaggerated and retained, leading to suboptimal acceleration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, most existing visual token pruning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> adopt a fixed pruning ratio, overlooking the substantial variation in semantic complexity and structural hierarchy across different scenes. In Fig. 1 (c), we compute the ratio of semantically important tokens under questions of different difficulty. It can be observed that, for simple questions that typically refer to a single object, the important visual tokens constitute only a minor portion of the overall visual representation, whereas complex questions involving multiple objects require more important visual tokens that carry essential semantic information. Therefore, using the same fixed pruning ratio across different scenes, which removes semantically critical tokens evenly, tends to be overly aggressive. It weakens inter-object contextual modeling and leads to incomplete visual understanding, thereby disrupting cross-modal reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address the above challenges, we present Efficient3D, a efficient visual token pruning framework designed to reduce the inference cost of 3D MLLMs. First, we introduce a Debiased Visual Token Importance Estimator (DVTIE) network. Unlike previous methods, our DVTIE removes the shallow initial layers when generating attention supervision, allowing for a more accurate estimation of visual token importance. As demonstrated in Fig. 1 (b), our DVTIE achieves better pruning accuracy, where pruning accuracy denotes the correctness of the predicted token-importance ranking. However, as more tokens are pruned, the remaining tokens tend to receive disproportionately higher attention from the LLM. Therefore, we introduce an Adaptive Token Rebalancing (ATR) strategy, where the adaptive scaling factor increases accordingly, applying stronger suppression to the attention weights of the remaining tokens to maintain balance. Comprehensive experiments show that Efficient3D achieves substantial inference acceleration and computational efficiency across multiple 3D scene understanding benchmarks, while maintaining performance comparable to the baseline models. In summary, our main contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the bottlenecks in visual token pruning for 3D MLLMs from biased attention in shallow initial layers, which undermines both the efficiency and reliability of inference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a DVTIE module to alleviate the influence from shallow initial layers, enabling efficient prediction of visual token importance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an ATR strategy that maintains attention balance when token pruning, preventing stronger suppression to the attention weights of retained tokens.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop the Efficient3D framework, which achieves state-of-the-art performance on multiple 3D vision-language benchmarks, <em id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, achieving a +2.57% improvement on the Scan2Cap dataset.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>3D Multi-modal Large Language Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> and Multi-modal LLMs (MLLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> stimulated research efforts to extend their reasoning capabilities to the 3D domain, which led to the emergence of 3D MLLMs for point cloud object understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and scene-level comprehension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>. Early studies rendered 3D environments into multi-view images to leverage 2D MLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, whereas subsequent approaches projected point clouds into the embedding space of LLMs using MLPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> or Q-formers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. More recent works, such as Chat-3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, Chat-Scene <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and 3DGraphLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite>, adopted object-centric paradigms to capture fine-grained spatial semantic relationships.
However, as these methods typically decompose a scene into hundreds of object-level visual tokens, they face significant computational and memory overhead during interface. To address this challenge, we propose Efficient3D, a adaptive token pruning framework that adaptively identifies and removes redundant tokens, thus improving efficiency while maintaining high-quality 3D multi-modal reasoning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02689v1/model_pipeline_upload.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/426;" width="685" height="426" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the Efficient3D framework.<span id="S2.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> First, we perform unpruned training on a pretrained 3D MLLM and extract importance scores of visual tokens.
Next, we use the visual importance scores as the supervision targets for training the proposed DVTIE. During inference, the 3D MLLM leverages the predicted importance scores from the DVTIE to perform visual token pruning. Furthermore, we propose an ATR strategy that adjusts the pruning ratio adaptively according to scene complexity.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Visual Token Compression</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual token compression had been extensively studied in transformer-based vision models such as ViTs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, primarily through pruning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, merging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, and skipping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite> techniques to reduce computational overhead. In MLLMs, prior studies mainly focused on efficient projector design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and dynamic token reduction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, but most approaches required additional fine-tuning of the base model. In contrast, recent training-free methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> were directly applicable to pre-trained MLLMs without parameter updates, making them more practical for deployment. Furthermore, some studies demonstrated that global attention maps aggregated from small-scale models could effectively guide token pruning in larger models, as exemplified by SGL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> and Fast3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. While previous approaches primarily focus on static token reduction, they often fail to generalize across diverse 3D scenes. We therefore introduce Efficient3D performs adaptive pruning of visual tokens during 3D MLLM inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 3D Multi-modal Large Language Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Efficient3D: A Unified Framework for Adaptive and Debiased Token Reduction in 3D MLLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> demonstrates our proposed visual token pruning framework, Efficient3D, which contains a Debiased Visual Token Importance Estimator network (DVTIE) to efficiently estimate token importance scores and an Adaptive Token Rebalancing strategy (ATR) for adaptive pruning based on the predicted token importance score from our DVTIE. The total token pruning pipeline can be summarized as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we train our DVTIE for visual token importance estimation. It takes the visual tokens and the prompt text tokens as input to predict the importance of each visual token. The aggregated attention matrix extracted from the 3D MLLM is used as the supervisory signal for learning the importance of visual tokens.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the DVTIE is trained, the predicted visual token importance is utilized to guide token pruning through the proposed ATR strategy, where it is combined with the cross-attention scores from each layer to effectively guide the pruning process layer by layer.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Debiased Visual Token Importance Estimator</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In 3D MLLM, the number of visual tokens is typically orders of magnitude larger than that of textual tokens, and thus redundant visual tokens can easily dominate the computational cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Besides, the cross attention mechanism can reflect the similarity between textual and visual features, and such similarity can be employed to indicate the relative contribution of visual tokens to the 3D MLLM’s output <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Therefore, we introduce the Debiased Visual Token Importance Estimator (DVTIE) network to learn a more accurate estimation of visual token importance based on the cross-attention weights from 3D MLLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 3D Multi-modal Large Language Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Efficient3D: A Unified Framework for Adaptive and Debiased Token Reduction in 3D MLLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b), given the input 3D point cloud and text prompt, we first follow Fast3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> to extract frozen visual embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denote the embedding length and channel size. Each visual embedding represents an object instance, integrating 3D geometric features and spatial positional information. Meanwhile, the text prompt is fed into BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to obtain textual embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{b}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the embedding length and channel size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, both the textual and visual embeddings are fed into a learnable linear projector, respectively, to align the channel size so as to get the projected textual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and visual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{proj}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{proj}</annotation></semantics></math> are refined through our <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> Dynamic Hybrid Compensation Attention Transformer (DHCT) layers, yielding the enhanced visual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{dy}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{dy}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and enabling progressive interaction between visual and textual features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In each DHCT layer, the visual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{proj}</annotation></semantics></math> is first fed into a spatial self-attention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> module, resulting in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{st}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{st}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represent the updated visual tokens after self-attention, enabling the DHCT to capture contextual dependencies among visual features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we set the visual features <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> as the query <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{q}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the textual features <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{proj}</annotation></semantics></math> as the key <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> and value <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling the model to capture high-rank cross modal semantics, as denoted:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{q}=F_{st}W_{Q},\quad T_{k}=T_{proj}W_{K},\quad T_{v}=T_{proj}W_{V}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{q}=F_{st}W_{Q},\quad T_{k}=T_{proj}W_{K},\quad T_{v}=T_{proj}W_{V},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable linear projectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The attention logits <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{high}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{high}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> are then computed by the standard scaled dot-product formulation:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{high}=\frac{F_{q}T_{k}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{p}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{high}=\frac{F_{q}T_{k}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{p}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{proj}</annotation></semantics></math> are fed into learnable projectors and mapped into a low-rank visual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> and textual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}_{proj}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the low-rank dimension, facilitating compact feature learning and highlighting informative cross-modal information, as denoted:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}_{proj}=F_{st}W_{Q}^{dy},\quad T^{\prime}_{proj}=T_{proj}W_{K}^{dy}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}_{proj}=F_{st}W_{Q}^{dy},\quad T^{\prime}_{proj}=T_{proj}W_{K}^{dy},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{low}=\sigma\!\left(\frac{F^{\prime}_{proj}{T^{\prime}_{proj}}^{\!\top}}{\sqrt{d^{\prime}}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><msqrt><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{low}=\sigma\!\left(\frac{F^{\prime}_{proj}{T^{\prime}_{proj}}^{\!\top}}{\sqrt{d^{\prime}}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{low}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{low}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the learnable low-rank association between visual and textual features. <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}^{dy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}^{dy}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K}^{dy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K}^{dy}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable linear projectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Then, <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{low}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{low}</annotation></semantics></math> is added with the attention logits <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{high}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{high}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>, which determines how each textual token attends to different visual tokens. The resulting attention scores <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are then used to weight the value vectors <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing the fused features <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{cs}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{cs}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the value vectors provide semantic context that guides the visual features toward textual features.
Subsequently, <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{cs}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{cs}</annotation></semantics></math> is passed through a feed-forward network (FFN) to obtain the refined feature <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{ffn}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{ffn}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which further enhances modality interaction. Then, the refined visual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{ffn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{ffn}</annotation></semantics></math> and the textual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{proj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{proj}</annotation></semantics></math> are fed into the next DHCT layer for further cross-modal interaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">After <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> layers DHCT module, we have enhanced visual feature <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{dy}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{dy}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we feed <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{dy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{dy}</annotation></semantics></math> into a MLP to produce predicted object-level attention score <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Target.</span>
To train the DVTIE, we first extract the attention maps from the 3D MLLM, which serve as supervision signals for learning token importance prediction.
Unlike Fast3D <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, our method aims to attenuate the interference of shallow initial layer attention on deep layer pruning decisions, thereby preventing the 3D MLLM from mistakenly preserving redundant visual tokens that no longer carry informative content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 3D Multi-modal Large Language Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Efficient3D: A Unified Framework for Adaptive and Debiased Token Reduction in 3D MLLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the concatenated visual tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and textual tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are fed into the 3D MLLM, where the attention matrix at layer <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Among the total <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers, we aggregate the attention matrices from layer <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the aggregated attention map, which reflects the integrated attention maps across multiple layers:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\tfrac{1}{L-K}\sum_{l=K}^{L}A^{(l)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\tfrac{1}{L-K}\sum_{l=K}^{L}A^{(l)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Next, from the aggregated attention map <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+M)\times(N+M)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+M)\times(N+M)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract three forms of object-level attention to assess the importance of visual tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
First, we extract the submatrix <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{vis}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{vis}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to attention among visual features and average <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{vis}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{vis}</annotation></semantics></math> along the query dimension to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{self}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{self}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we extract the submatrix <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{pro}\in\mathbb{R}^{M_{p}\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pro}\in\mathbb{R}^{M_{p}\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to attention from prompt features to visual features and average <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{pro}</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> prompt features to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{prompt}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{prompt}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of prompt tokens, i.e., the tokens corresponding to the task instructions.
Finally, we extract the submatrix <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{text}\in\mathbb{R}^{M_{c}\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{text}\in\mathbb{R}^{M_{c}\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to attention from caption features to visual features and average <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{text}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{text}</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> feature tokens to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{text}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{text}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of caption tokens, i.e., the tokens corresponding to the textual caption or description in 3D scenes.
The training target attention <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted as:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=a_{self}+a_{prompt}+a_{text}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=a_{self}+a_{prompt}+a_{text}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss Function.</span>
To optimize the DVTIE, we adopt a multi-objective loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> that jointly enforces rank consistency and attention fidelity for reliable token pruning. First, to strictly preserve rank consistency, we introduce the following ranking loss function:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{rank}=\sum_{i,j}\mathbf{1}(a_{i}&gt;a_{j})\cdot\max(0,\hat{a}_{j}-\hat{a}_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{rank}=\sum_{i,j}\mathbf{1}(a_{i}&gt;a_{j})\cdot\max(0,\hat{a}_{j}-\hat{a}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p14.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}(a_{i}&gt;a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}(a_{i}&gt;a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is an indicator function that equals 1 when the target scores satisfy <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}&gt;a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}&gt;a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and 0 otherwise. Note that <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> denote the indices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, we employ the KL divergence loss to measure the discrepancy between the predicted and target attention distributions, ensuring attention fidelity.
By combining KL divergence loss with the rank consistency constraint, the final loss function is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\text{KL}(a\parallel\hat{a})+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{rank}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\text{KL}(a\parallel\hat{a})+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{rank},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p15.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling factor that balances the magnitude of the loss components.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Adaptive Token Rebalancing Strategy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since fixed ratio visual token pruning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> often overlook the significant variations in semantic complexity and structural hierarchy across different scenes, we propose an Adaptive Token Rebalancing (ATR) strategy.
ATR performs adaptive pruning of visual tokens during 3D MLLM inference, avoiding excessive pruning and semantic loss in simple scenes while preserving more critical tokens in complex scenes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the 3D MLLM inference stage, the visual tokens <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and textual tokens <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are jointly fed into the 3D MLLM to establish cross-modal representations.
Subsequently, the target attention distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> predicted by the DVTIE is employed to guide the adaptive pruning procedure.
Specifically, starting from the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer of the 3D MLLM, we progressively accumulate the attention scores of each visual object,
where the attention scores are obtained by averaging the cross modal attention maps over attention heads.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">First, we denote the attention scores obtained at the current layer as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
During the adaptive pruning process, as certain visual tokens are progressively pruned, the number of tokens dynamically changes,
which may cause a distributional shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> in the attention modeling of the remaining tokens. To mitigate this issue, we introduce a ”shadow factor” <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which adaptively scales the attention scores <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the adjusted attention <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
thereby maintaining the stability of the attention distribution throughout the pruning process, as denoted:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}=\sum_{i}\hat{a}\cdot I_{k-1,i},\quad p^{\prime}_{k}=p_{k}\cdot(1-s_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}=\sum_{i}\hat{a}\cdot I_{k-1,i},\quad p^{\prime}_{k}=p_{k}\cdot(1-s_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k-1}\in\{0,1\}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k-1}\in\{0,1\}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the pruning token mask of the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k{-}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k{-}1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer.
If <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k-1,i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k-1,i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, it indicates that the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th visual token has been pruned,
conversely, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k-1,i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k-1,i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> means that the token is retained for subsequent computations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The adjusted attention <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is then integrated into the cumulative attention estimation, so that when more important tokens are progressively pruned, the subsequent accumulation proceeds more conservatively, preventing over-pruning and stabilizing the adaptive pruning dynamics, as denoted:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}=C_{k-1}+\frac{p^{\prime}_{k}}{L-K}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}=C_{k-1}+\frac{p^{\prime}_{k}}{L-K},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cumulative attention at the <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Subsequently, we compares the cumulative attention <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with the target attention distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math> in an element-wise manner.
When the cumulative attention of the <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th visual token satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k,i}\geq\hat{a}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k,i}\geq\hat{a}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
, the corresponding mask <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k,i}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k,i}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }C_{k,i}\geq\hat{a}_{i},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k,i}=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }C_{k,i}\geq\hat{a}_{i},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Once updated, the pruning mask <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to construct the attention mask for the <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer of the 3D MLLM, progressively suppressing redundant visual tokens and enabling attention accumulated adaptive pruning for efficient 3D MLLM inference acceleration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Adaptive Token Rebalancing</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Require :</span> 3D MLLM <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>; tokens <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V,T</annotation></semantics></math>; attention distribution <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}</annotation></semantics></math>;start <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return :</span> <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Init:</span> <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k-1}\!\leftarrow\!\mathbf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k-1}\!\leftarrow\!\mathbf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k-1}\!\leftarrow\!\mathbf{0}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k-1}\!\leftarrow\!\mathbf{0}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. when <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> = <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}\leftarrow{A}_{T\rightarrow V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}\leftarrow{A}_{T\rightarrow V}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross modal attention map.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}\leftarrow\sum_{i}\hat{a}_{i}I_{k-1,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}\leftarrow\sum_{i}\hat{a}_{i}I_{k-1,i}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> scales attention accumulation according to previous pruning mask.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}_{k}\leftarrow p_{k}(1-s_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}_{k}\leftarrow p_{k}(1-s_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>,
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{k}\leftarrow C_{k-1}+\dfrac{p^{\prime}_{k}}{L-K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{k}\leftarrow C_{k-1}+\dfrac{p^{\prime}_{k}}{L-K}</annotation></semantics></math>,
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.4.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_upright">to</span> <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.4.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{k,i}\leftarrow\mathbf{1}[C_{k,i}\geq\hat{a}_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k,i}\leftarrow\mathbf{1}[C_{k,i}\geq\hat{a}_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> where<math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k,i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k,i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the <math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th visual token is pruned.
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
            construct <math id="algorithm1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="algorithm1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="algorithm1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the attention mask for layer <math id="algorithm1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <em id="algorithm1.4.10.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math></em>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
